  /* The printed year exists on paper and nowhere else. */
  .printed {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
    Printed, this is the year as a dated list and nothing else (#236, #271).

    A school office prints this page to pin the year up, and what it wants
    pinned up is the artefact it already maintains by hand: a masthead, then
    every date carrying something, month by month, two columns down the sheet.
    So the header, the footer and the buttons come off, and so do the page's
    own three bands — the heading band, which the masthead replaces; the
    subscribe block, which offers a printed sheet a link it cannot follow; and
    the month grid, which is nine sheets of mostly empty boxes. The two semester
    sheets used to come off here too; they are deleted (#237).

    **The format is the reference's and the typography is this site's** (#271).
    The underlined month headings and the two columns come from the sheet the
    school supplied; the faces, the navy, the en dash between two dates and the
    way a time is written do not, because a family reading paper and a family
    reading the screen are reading one calendar.

    Everything here is print-only: nothing in this block can change what the
    page looks like on a screen.
  */
  @media print {
    /*
      Three whole bands, not the parts of them that looked like chrome. Each
      band's heading and standfirst describe a thing paper does not have — a
      grid with panels to open, an address to subscribe to — and a heading left
      behind over nothing is worse than the band it came from.
    */
    [data-section='calendar-header'],
    [data-section='calendar-subscribe'],
    [data-section='calendar-months'] {
      display: none;
    }

    .printed {
      display: block;
    }

    /*
      The masthead: the mark, the school's name, and the year the sheet is of.

      Centred, which is the one place this sheet is centred — the reference's
      shape, and the shape of every school calendar pinned up beside it. The
      rule under it separates the sheet's identity from its dates.
    */
    .printed-masthead {
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
      padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
      border-bottom: 1.5pt solid var(--color-navy);
      color: var(--color-navy);
    }

    /*
      0.86in, measured off the prototype the look was settled in. Small enough
      that the year still fits on one sheet, large enough that the trace reads
      across a hallway.
    */
    .printed-mark {
      width: 0.86in;
      height: auto;
      /* The site's reset makes an image a block, so the masthead's
         `text-align` reaches the two lines of type and not the mark. */
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .printed-wordmark {
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 9pt;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.22em;
      /* The tracking is applied to the right of each letter, including the
         last; the negative margin gives the line back its optical centre. */
      margin: 0.35rem 0 0 0.22em;
    }

    .printed-title {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: 17pt;
      font-weight: 400;
      line-height: 1.15;
      margin-top: 0.15rem;
    }

    /*
      Two columns, and the browser keeps a month whole where it can. A month
      broken across the fold of a column is the one thing a pinned-up sheet is
      read wrong from — the eye finds the heading and reads the wrong dates
      under it.

      One sheet is what this arrangement is *for* and not what it promises: no
      rule here forces it, because the only ways to force it are a font size
      nobody reads and dropping dates the school actually has.
    */
    .printed-columns {
      columns: 2;
      column-gap: 2rem;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 10.5pt;
      line-height: 1.35;
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }

    .printed-month {
      break-inside: avoid;
      margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    }

    /*
      Underlined month headings, which is the reference's own device and the one
      typographic convention on it this sheet does copy. Set in the site's serif
      and its navy rather than the reference's faces: the format is what was
      asked for, and a second set of faces on a school's own artefact is how
      paper starts looking like somebody else's school.
    */
    .printed-month h3 {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: 12pt;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--color-navy);
      border-bottom: 0.75pt solid var(--color-navy);
      padding-bottom: 0.1rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }

    .printed-month li {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      break-inside: avoid;
      padding: 0.05rem 0;
    }

    /*
      The dates in their own column, so the sheet reads down the days rather
      than down a ragged left edge — which is what the school's own hand-kept
      calendar does. The width is a floor and not a cap: a bare "3" still lines
      up under a "25–26", and a run that crossed a month and reads "30
      November–1 December" takes the room it needs rather than wrapping.

      Quieter than what it dates, because on this sheet the day number is the
      index and the thing that happens is the entry.
    */
    .printed-days {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      min-width: 2.2rem;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      color: var(--color-ink-2);
    }

    /*
      A term boundary in the heavier weight, as the reference sets its own first
      day of school. It is the one line on the sheet that says what the *year*
      does rather than what one date carries, and a family scanning for when
      classes start finds it without reading the closures.
    */
    .printed-term .printed-what {
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--color-navy);
    }
  }
